From d881bc036e96b085ce6ed34637eb60d0f0e071c6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 23 Jul 2026 22:06:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CI: run test_twin_live.py too, via a native Postgres instead
 of the Docker service

The remaining honest gap flagged in the twin-live-infra commit: GitHub
Actions' services: postgres: block is a separate Docker container reached
only over TCP, and test_twin_live.py's whole design depends on peer auth --
an OS user connecting to Postgres over a Unix domain socket the two share.
There is no socket to share with a containerized service, so no amount of
test-side fixing could ever make it pass against that setup.

Fix: replace the services: postgres: container with a natively
apt-installed Postgres on the runner itself (same package, same default
pg_hba.conf 'local all all peer' rule this repo's own dev containers use),
provisioned with the matching iceberg/iceberg superuser + database, then
provision the twin suite's OS identities via the now-committed
scripts/twin_ensure_services.sh before running the full suite with no
exclusions at all. Redis keeps its existing Docker service container --
it has no peer-auth-style identity boundary to preserve, so TCP is fine.

Verification method and its limits, stated plainly: I cannot run a real
GitHub Actions job from this environment, so this is not verified the way
everything else delivered this session was. What I did instead: extracted
every run: block from the actual workflow YAML and replayed them, in
order, in a freshly-purged environment (Postgres, Redis, and all three OS
identities removed entirely, not just reset) using a small local sudo
stub that faithfully preserves flag semantics (-u, -E) so the commands
executing are byte-identical to what the workflow specifies -- this is
the same base OS GitHub's ubuntu-latest runner uses. Result: full suite,
no exclusions, 383/383. One step ('Install dependencies', plain
